    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Amplification of fibroblast growth factor receptor 2 (FGFR2) has been demonstrated to correlate with bone, skin and cancer pathologies. A recent study identified a particular single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) in an intronic region of FGFR2 that is associated with increased risk of breast cancer in Chinese women using oral contraceptives. However, the molecular mechanism for the impact of this SNP on the expression of FGFR2 is unknown. The data presented here proposes that this particular intronic region contains an enhancer for the FGFR2 gene which, when mutated, amplifies FGFR2 expression, potentially leading to disease. Using cultured human skin cells as a model for epithelial tissue, we examined the enhancer activity of the SNP-containing region via an enhancer reporter assay. \r\nWe found that when the SNP was present, the enhancer demonstrated significantly amplified activity in keratinocytes as compared to wild-type. Analysis of the transcription factor binding motifs present in the region suggests that the wild-type region binds HLTF, a transcription factor which can be activating or repressive, whereas the mutated region instead binds MYC, a highly activating transcription factor. Further research will seek to confirm these protein-DNA interactions. If confirmed, this interaction could serve as a potential therapeutic target for women suffering from breast cancer caused by oral contraceptive usage.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
